\f1\fs22 Sarah settled into the chair behind the desk at the back of the Ironing Room and activated both integrated consoles. A distinctive perfume announced itself to Sarah\'92s left. Eloise had decided to attend run.\
\'93Oh!\'94 Sarah tried to life herself out of the chair.\
\'93Stay there,\'94 Eloise said. \'93The command post is yours.\'94\
The Johnny looked back and saw Eloise loitering, and rushed to take her a chair. Whether his rush had thrown him off balance, or he\'92d simply underestimated the weight of it, Johnny tripped and coughed the chair forward. Chip stepped over the top of him, lifted the chair in one hand and delivered to Eloise.\
\'93Ma\'92am,\'94 he said deferentially.\
\'93Thank you, Chip.\'94 Eloise sat, rolled the chair closer to Sarah, crossed her legs, and placed her hand bag on the floor by her side. \'93It\'92s taken me weeks to get a ticket to this,\'94 Eloise said to Sarah.\
\'93You should have told me,\'94 Sarah answered. \'93I know people. I could have got you in any time.\'94\
\'93I don\'92t trust friends in high places,\'94 Eloise cracked.\
The team booted the washing machine and initialised the test program. It came alive and slew around to a new position on it azimuth.\
\'93Has the Cosmic Microwave Background moved?\'94 Eloise asked.\
Sarah rolled her eyes, knowing that rolling eyes at Eloise was an exclusion on the company\'92s life assurance plan. \'93It\'92s a week since the last test, different time of day. We\'92ve moved.\'94\
\'93I see,\'94 Eloise said.\
Sarah was sure Eloise did see, and saw well before put her lipstick on that morning. \'93Mostly in circles, if understand it.\'94\
\'93Oh well, that\'92s business.\'94\
Dr Quark was ordering kebab speeds wth more confidence.\
\'93Five monkeys swinging on the bar,\'94 a technician announced.\
Sarah watched the monkeys travel around the elliptical rail while travelling above and below teh plain of the ellipse as the went. The secondary arms rotated like fan blades, and the kebab harnesses flipped their coins faster than the eye could see. The entire machine was smoother, more in harmony with itself, as if it had been playing jazz with broken instruments last time, and had transformed into an orchestra inside a week.\
The overhead view of the washing machine switched to a thematic that plotted only the kebabs with vapour trails aiding human comprehension with what it saw.\
\'93It\'92s all quite beautiful,\'94 Eloise said. \'93Like whirling dervishes dancing Swan Lake.\'94\
Sarah thought that was a pretty good analogy.\
\'93I\'92m proud of you, Sarah, and I may have softened some of my contempt for Johnny. If he hadn\'92t kept buying himself in with more discs\'85 and this new machine as very impressive.\'94\
\'93We can thank your engineers for that.\'94 Sarah gave Eloise side-eye. \'93I\'92m sure you are used to them rising  to teh occasion on other projects like this.\'94\
\'93There are no projects like this.\'94\
The two women fell silent while they digested their intuitions.\
The monkeys had settled into relatively static places on the rail while they gently waved up and down around their pivots on the rail. The camera angle from the top showed them maintaining a pentagram, more or less, stretched by the constraints of the ellipse. If anything, the monkeys were slowly drifting ant-clockwise.\
The worms on the kebab speed graph climbed. All five representing the actual speeds them stayed green.\
Chilo wandered behind technicians, watching their screens. Kleo sat on a stool to the side and in front of the stations, watching the people in the room and much as the diagnostics on the on the front wall. Solo leaned on a desk scanned the room for dissent while nibbling on sesame-and-honey cake.\
Chip was looking for signs of danger and, failing those, reasons he could shut the test down. That everything was going so smoothly was no comfort to him.\
The nominal worm rose again. The five other went white, followed the nominal and reverted to green when the came within reach. The six lines became a tight braid.\
One other the technicians put a coffee cup down on the precipice of their desk, Sarah noticed; so did Chip.\
The monkeys started to increase their anti-clockwise crabbing. Within a minute they were completing circuits of the track in a little over a second.\
\'93Here we go,\'94 Johnny said with a little  glee.\
\'93Keep alert,\'94 Chip warned.\
\'93Eyes on your data,\'94 Dr Quark advised.\
The monkeys kept lapping faster. One of the technicians started calling the revolutions per sec. \'93Two point four.\'94\
\'93Maglev stable,\'94 another said.\
The monkeys began to flicker and Sarah couldn\'92t discern one from the other.\
\'93Four point seven.\'94\
\'93Shut it down,\'94 Chip ordered.\
\'93Belay that,\'94 Johnny ordered.\
The technicians trusted Dr Quark more than Johnny; they were too frightened of Chip to decide whether they trusted him or not.\
\'93Nine point three.\'94\
Then the secondary arms increased their revolutions by the square of something. The technician watching those numbers didn\'92t get time to announce it.\
The monkeys began to slow\'85\
\'85as the whole washing machine began to slew on its azimuth track.\
\'93Who\'92s doing that?\'94 Johnny blurted.\
\'93Stop the test,\'94 Chip reiterated. He turned and made puppy eyes at Sarah and Eloise. \'93It\'92s operating outside expecting parameters,\'94 he said as he advanced on them.\
Dr Quark joined them at the back of the room. \'93It\'92s finding a new level of stability.\'94\
\'93I am aware of that,\'94 Chip countered.\
Eloise looked Chip straight in the face. \'93It seems to me, Mr Stockwell, you claim it\'92s dangerous when it\'92s unstable, and it\'92s it\'92s dangerous when it\'92s not.\'94\
\'93It\'92s dangerous when it\'92s on, and it\'92s more dangerous than it was five minutes ago.\'94\
\'93Proceed,\'94 Eloise told Dr Quark.\
Chip locked eyes with Sarah.\
\'93I need your oversight on this project,\'94 Eloise said to Sarah.\
Sarah swallowed and nodded to Dr Quark, who spun on his feet at went back to his station.\
The machine cycled on its track a little fast than once a second as its primary arms stopped running around the rail. They were still waving independently of each other. If they had been holding threads, they might have been weaving something.\
Sarah closed her eyes.\
Little Clinton moved.\
Sarah opened her eyes.\
Chip rubbed his hands down his face.\
One of the technicians spoke. \'93Dr Quark? I\'92ve got something else.\'94\
Quark crossed to her desk, looked, and tapped a screen. A new display occupied part of the right-hand panel. \
Short bars appeared on there, vanished, then returned in almost the same places. More followed. The display rearranged them, shuffled them.\
\'93It\'92s a signal,\'94 a technician said. \'93Coherent. There\'92s information in it.\'94\
\'93Can you parse it?\'94 Dr. Quark asked.\
The technician looked puzzled. \'93It\'92s encrypted.\'94\
Dr Quark didn\'92t need to ask the technician how she could know that. 
\f2\i Encrypted
\f1\i0  meant teh system was recognisable. Dr Quark turned to other technician. \'93Can you decrypt it?\'94\
The technical look at his boss like he\'92s just asked if he could tie his shoelaces.\
\'93We\'92re Shakti,\'94 the technician said, and got to work.\
Lines accumulated across the wall and the decryption algorithms got to work. Lines grouped, separated, some disappeared, new ones appeared. Sarah knew the light show was, at best, a pantomime if what the software was doing, but it had the room holding its breath. At least it felt like it was making progress. Repeated structures began to hold their places.\
The overhead schematic of the washing machine showed it still running laps on it track.\
The decryption pantomime halted and faded.\
\'93Sir, we have a message.\'94\
\'93On screen,\'94 Johnny directed.\
All of the schematics and telemetry on the front wall flicked away and was replaced with a single sentence:\
